{"agent":"q1-kilo","data":{"by":"grok-4.7","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"A single item that is both INTERRUPTED and already merged is emitted as `recover` before `complete`, and both actions are returned. Given STALL with `item.started`, an expired lease, and `worktree.merged` sha `a1b2c3d4e5f67890abcdef12` while `terminal` is still false, the first loop appends `Act(\"recover\", \"STALL\", ...)` and the second also appends `Act(\"complete\", \"STALL\", ...)`. A one-step driver therefore runs `ddflow recover` on work that already merged, which the docstring itself says is meant to salvage uncommitted crash work before anything starts from scratch, instead of `ddflow complete`. Recover should be skipped once `merged_sha` is set.","digest":"bfd892a35fe4735b","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/orchestrator.py:monitor","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/orchestrator.py:monitor"},{"chunk":1,"detail":"Same-tier acts are raw `state.items` insertion order, not value order, so the \"next\" action is wrong whenever more than one item matches. Log an INTERRUPTED item `LOW` (priority 1) before `HIGH` (priority 90), both with no live lease: `monitor` returns `recover LOW` first because `for iid, it in state.items.items()` never applies `order_key` / `fill_order`. A coordinator that takes `acts[0]` salvages the cheap item and leaves the high-priority crash unrecovered. The same unordered walk applies to the complete loop.","digest":"694682203afa910f","family":"xai","location":"ddflow/services/orchestrator.py:monitor","malformed":"","reviewer":"grok-4.7","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/orchestrator.py:monitor"}],"chunks_total":1,"coverage":"1/1 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":7512,"diff_sha":"38924d6e21c9eca76708aeff7b059f78e5f1459042a79c7cbb04371e4315c5d1","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in $HOME/src/ddflow/.ddflow/worktrees/fix-Bd43790a326","diff_sta